<!--
     The panel scaler (DSU) offers 1440x2960 and 1080x2220. The display has no identification
     data, so DisplayDeviceConfig finds this file by its port (port 0). Densities as stock:
     ro.sf.init.lcd_density=560 (native), ro.sf.lcd_density=420 (FHD+), stock build.prop:124-125.
-->
<displayConfiguration>
    <densityMapping>
        <density>
            <height>2220</height>
            <width>1080</width>
            <density>420</density>
        </density>
        <density>
            <height>2960</height>
            <width>1440</width>
            <density>560</density>
        </density>
    </densityMapping>
    <!--
         Backlight -> luminance. LightsService sends round(1 + 254 * value) to the Samsung lights
         HAL, which writes value * 36600 / 255 to the panel (max_brightness = BRT(366)); the panel
         driver uses level ceil(brightness / 100) (BRT_TBL_INDEX, stock panel_bl.h). Each point is
         one value the HAL can send: levels 0..255 take the stock framework-res
         config_screenBrightnessNits, the HBM levels (256..365) the panel's own candela table
         (/sys/class/lcd/panel/brightness_table). The map reaches HAL value 254 = level 365, stock's
         config_screenBrightnessExtendedMaximum, which the framework maximum of the overlay
         (config_screenBrightnessSettingMaximumFloat = 0.996063) also is; the closing point at HAL
         value 255 = level 366 (720 nits) is never requested. HAL values 178..195 (levels 256..280)
         sit on the panel's 420-nit step but are interpolated towards 465: a second 420-nit point
         would break the strictly increasing nits that the nits-to-backlight spline needs
         (Spline.createSpline).
